Abstract
The need of the aerospace industry, at national or European level, of faster yet reliable computational fluid dynamics models is the main drive for the application of model reduction techniques. This need is linked to the time cost of high-fidelity models, rendering them inefficient for applications like multi-disciplinary optimization. With the goal of testing and applying model reduction to computational fluid dynamics models applicable to lifting surfaces, a bibliographical research covering reduction of nonlinear, dynamic, or steady models was conducted. This established the prevalence of projection and least mean squares methods, which rely on solutions of the original high-fidelity model and their proper orthogonal decomposition to work. Other complementary techniques such as adaptive sampling, greedy sampling, and hybrid models are also presented and discussed. These projection and least mean squares methods are then tested on simple and documented benchmarks to estimate the error and speed-up of the reduced order models thus generated. Dynamic, steady, nonlinear, and multiparametric problems were reduced, with the simplest version of these methods showing the most promise. These methods were later applied to single parameter problems, namely the lid-driven cavity with incompressible Navier–Stokes equations and varying Reynolds number, and the elliptic airfoil at varying angles of attack for compressible Euler flow. An analysis of the performance of these methods is given at the end of this article, highlighting the computational speed-up obtained with these techniques, and the challenges presented by multiparametric problems and problems showing point singularities in their domain.
Introduction
Under the European NOVEMOR project, an optimization framework was created that allows for the multidisciplinary optimization (MDO) of airplanes with morphing aerodynamic surfaces.1 In this framework the aerodynamics of a wing is modeled using a panel code with skin friction and compressibility corrections to account for the viscous drag and transonic Mach (0.6–0.8) numbers present in normal commercial aircraft cruise conditions. The panel code was complemented by a surrogate model that added the aerodynamic forces due to the stabilizing planes and fuselage. These models allow for a simple computation of the drag and lift of the aircraft without being too costly to calculate, being well adapted to optimization routines that require frequent model computations.2 However, these simplified models and surrogate models are limited in their predicting power. Surrogate models are so limited due to the fact that they do not incorporate the “physics” of the problem: a set of parametrized curves is merely made to fit the given data. Simplified models are limited because the approximations that they are based on necessarily limit or misrepresent the underlying physics. For the aerodynamics module, the potential model with Prandtl–Glauert correction used by the authors ceases to be accurate for higher Mach numbers or when in presence of transonic shocks (which are not captured by potential flow theory). This is quite critical in the optimization of transonic aircraft as the appearance of shocks at subsonic speeds severely hampers the fuel economy of an airplane. To properly capture transonic shocks, a compressible Euler code or compressible Reynolds averaged Navier–Stokes (RANS) code should be used. These higher-fidelity models or high-definition models (HDMs) would be able to properly predict the very complicated phenomena that must be accounted for when designing a commercial airplane, but they may take far too much time to compute once inside an optimization loop. Ideally there would be a way to create a simpler and faster model that still captures the relevant physics encoded in the HDM.
One method not yet fully explored is model order reduction (MOR).3 MOR takes the partial differential equation (PDE) or ordinary differential equation (ODE) described by the HDM and generates a reduced order model (ROM) that is essentially another, smaller, equation set with less unknowns. This ODE is supposed to be much faster to compute while generating results not very different from the HDM. In other words, the point of MOR is to take the HDM and generate a model that is faster to compute but still captures the same physics of the HDM.
This possibility of obtaining a result closer to reality with smaller computation times has led both the industry and the scientific community to investigate MOR. However, the complexity of the models used in the aerospace industry means that there are no readily available software packages for creating and computing ROMs.
Lucia et al.4 provide a review on ROM with specific focus on Volterra series, proper orthogonal decomposition (POD), and harmonic balance for computational aeroelasticity and unsteady aerodynamics applications. Chinesta et al.5 and Benner et al.6 presented overviews of proper generalization decomposition (a priori) and projection-based reduction (a posteriori) methods, respectively. More recently, Lu et al.7 provided an extensive review of POD methods. Nayfeh et al.8 presented an overview of ROMs to be applied for studying MEMS devices. In Lassila et al.9 the authors present a survey of ROM methods, with special attention to Galerkin-projection ROMs, for incompressible fluid dynamics, considering both steady and unsteady cases. An overview of ROM for large scale systems was done by Antoulas et al.10 Some test cases were presented including structural, building, heat, CD player, clamped beam, and Butterworth filter models. ROMs for both linear and nonlinear problems were reviewed by Baur et al.11 Ghoreyshi et al.12 reviewed ROMs (including Volterra series, radial basis function, and surrogate models) for stability and control analysis that use computational fluid dynamics (CFD). A recent book covering several ROM methods and providing a tutorial to ROMs was edited by Benner et al.13 Also recently, Yondo et al.2 provided a useful survey covering the broad topic of surrogate models for aerodynamic analyses, where the authors also provide the advances in MOR alongside other methods.
ANSYS14 has recently presented their own efforts and interest in model reduction. The primary applications discussed involve speeding up simulation time and design space exploration; to create real-time models for control, monitoring, and diagnostics; to enhance the simulation of heterogeneous systems, and thus model-based systems engineering. The company itself displayed its recent progress with MOR and its proprietary CFD software: FLUENT. For a test case of flow under the hood of a car, with a k–ε turbulence model, it was shown how a ROM generated a result with a maximum error in pressure of 1.2% relative to the HDM. In terms of speed-up, the ROM has taken 3 s to run on a laptop, and the HDM took 2 h on a 16-core cluster.14 A leave-one-out error estimator was used to estimate ROM error.
A recent effort by researchers in Prague and Hamburg led to the development of a MOR tool for simpleFOAM, a steady state-solver available in OpenFOAM for incompressible flows, featuring turbulence modeling.15 The authors used the discrete empirical interpolation method (see Chaturantabut and Sorensen16 for another description of the method), a hyper-reduction method based on the Galerkin projection and POD. The thus developed tool was used to optimize the gas flow speed in a separation column, used by the oil industry to separate the various species in crude oil.
Another tool for OpenFOAM, called ITHACA-FV,17,18 is in its early development at the Mathlab of SISSA, in Trieste, Italy. As part of the European AROMA-CFD project, it attempts to provide several reduced order modeling techniques to be used on parametric problems in OpenFOAM.
The European program AEROGUST for aircraft robustness against wind gusts19 funded several MOR efforts: these include MOR in the time domain by Fourier decomposition, developed by NUMECA;20 a hybrid technique, developed by Optimad Engineering, and later applied to the shape optimization of the front bumper of a car,21 which was almost 4 × faster than traditional CFD optimization with an error of 0.49%; a free-form deformation method with POD-based ROMs was tested to optimize the aerodynamic shape of a car's front bumper22 and a sailing boat23; the least mean squares method with POD, developed by the DLR, which is employed in their aircraft multidisciplinary design optimizer to find the critical load cases in the aircraft's flight envelope (and thus, size its structure)24 including gust loads (considering unsteady aerodynamics).25 The DLR has also researched a method using Isomap for basis creation.26,27 In Ripepi et al.,27 the efforts carried out by DLR under the project Digital-X on MOR are summarized, which include POD- and Isomap-based ROMs.
Other research efforts include: the reduced basis methods and the surrounding community;28,29 proper generalized decomposition (PGD) which was partly developed by EADS (now Airbus Group) researchers5,30,31 and has been applied to composite structures; and MOR for satellite thermal problems being developed by NASA.32
These technologies are still being perfected and the main issues to solve in this area are “certification” or finding accurate measures of ROM error without solving the HDM; how to obtain reliable ROMs for nonlinear parametric problems (such as aerodynamics); how to integrate a ROM in an automatic optimizer, since data or designs initially used to train a ROM may be very different from the true optimal design.
The point of this work is to present a comprehensive review of several MOR methods, together with specific test cases and with a special focus on aerodynamic applications and their applicability to CFD. The objectives can then be listed as such:
•	
Research available model reduction methods and ascertain the state of the art in MOR;
•	
Test chosen model reduction methods on simple problems exhibiting properties similar to CFD models;
•	
Test chosen model reduction methods on simple CFD models with physical phenomena present in modern aircraft;
•	
Conclude on the applicability of MOR to CFD of lifting surfaces and its eventual use in MDO.
Hence the goal is to understand the error and speed-up generated by the use of existing MOR techniques, in particular for aerodynamic models. In other words, the goal is to study the error and speed of ROMs relative to the high-fidelity models from which they were created. This work, coupled with other literature of this area and related to the HDMs themselves should allow the reader to quantify how much faster a ROM is relative to a real-life testing campaign and how far apart ROM's results are from reality.
Background
This section presents the bibliographical research done on MOR methods, in particular their theory, the state of the art, and how they can be applied in typical industry problems. The section is organized as follows:
First, model reduction methods, which replace the original HDM problem or equations by another, reduced problem or equation set, are shown and discussed. Second, basis creation, or how to create the basis for the reduced solution subspace, is presented. Then, error estimation and control deals with the estimation of error for the models discussed. Subsequently, sampling, or how to extract data from the HDM, so as to obtain the best reduced basis possible, is treated. Finally techniques that do not fit the previous definitions are shown. These techniques are used to decrease the error of the ROM and are presented in the “Precision” subsection.
Model reduction methods
A posteriori methods. These methods rely on previous full-order calculations so as to generate the required reduced basis. A distinction is made between basis generation and model reduction methods, because basis generation does not necessarily determine the new equations to be solved, but their coefficients. This distinction is also useful to understand the literature around model reduction with POD and detect similarities between the several methods.
Projection-based reduction methods for PDEs are based on the following concept: take the following semi-discretized (as in discretized by finite elements, finite volumes, or finite differences) parametrized PDE system in the form33,34
dw(μ)dt=f(w(μ),μ)
(1)
y=g(w(μ))
(2)
Here 
w∈ℝn
 (n being the order of the HDM) is the HDM state vector, μ is the parameter space vector, f is the system function, g is the output function, and y are the system outputs. Though a dynamic system is shown, the following ideas can be applied to a steady system too. Given a n × k matrix V, called the reduced basis, and reduced state space vector 
q∈ℝk
 (k being the order of the ROM with k < n), such that
Vq≈w
(3)
V is then a basis for the subspace of order k in 
ℝn
 that would well approximate the trajectory of w, meaning the space occupied by w as the time or parameters vary. Considering the above assumption and a residual term r to account for this approximation equation (1) can be rewritten as
dVq(μ)dt=f(Vq(μ),μ)+r(μ,t)
(4)
The residue 
r∈ℝn
 needs to be eliminated in order to have a system that only depends on q. Suppose another n × k matrix W, such that
WTr(μ,t)=0
(5)
The reduced order system can be written as
dq(μ)dt=(WTV)-1WTf(Vq(μ),μ)
(6)
y=g(Vq(μ))
(7)
This is called the Petrov–Galerkin projection. The equations are projected onto a subspace that should capture well the dynamics of the complete solution, in a direction parallel to the residue due to the use of such a subspace. One intuitive notion of this method can be found in aircraft performance and flight modes courses taught in university: Dutch roll can be considered to be a horizontal motion, with acceptable error in period (less than 10%).35 In this case the equations of motion of the aircraft were “projected” onto the horizontal plane of motion, producing a model with less unknowns than before and giving a solution with acceptable error. For V = W, one obtains the so-called Galerkin projection.
For CFD, even though we solve a stationary problem, a time marching method might be used to find the answer. Therefore, model reduction techniques that deal with dynamical systems are also explored in this article.
Unfortunately, projection-based methods require to a certain degree to tamper with the solver. This is because the equation to solve is no longer 
f()=0
 but 
(WTV)-1WTf()=0
.
An evolution of these methods, hyper-reduction, is a Galerkin or Petrov–Galerkin projection where only certain entries of f are evaluated and the rest of the solution is reconstructed from these entries.34,36 These methods are more complex than those presented before and require full-access to the source code of a model. An overview of projection-based reduction methods is provided by Benner et al.6
Least mean squares reduction, in theory is quite simple and presents many variants,37–39 but only the version in Iollo et al.38 will be shown. For a steady system it is posed as follows
q=argminq||f(Vq,μ)||
(8)
Therefore, rather than projecting the system of equations in such a way that one can neglect 
r(t,μ)
, the residue for a given basis V is minimized. Rather than minimizing the error over all reduced state space, one can try to minimize the residue at a boundary as in Iollo et al.,38 add regularizations or even attempt to optimize the base V.39 This method has been applied with satisfactory results for 2D Euler (see Iollo et al.38 and LeGresley and Alonso40) and 3D RANS equations (see Ripepi and Görtz41).
Reduction by L1-norm minimization was developed in Abgrall et al.42 specifically for hyperbolic equations and is based on their discretization by the finite volume method. For a steady problem, the ROM is


argminq||f(Vq,μ)||11
(9)
See Abgrall et al.42 for other variants and the method for dynamic systems. For the 1D Euler equations, the authors found the precision of the solution to be enhanced if q is calculated separately for each physical quantity being evaluated (velocity, pressure, etc.). The article is recent (published in 2016) and the authors are currently trying to extend the method to the multidimensional case and are developing an error measure.
A priori methods—The PGD. PGD has been used for uncertainty quantification in several domains, including structural calculations (see Chinesta et al.5 and Chevreuil and Nouy43 or Chinesta et al.30 for a discussion on the matter). More recently, it has been applied to the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations in Dumon et al.44 for a lid-driven cavity problem, with a speed-up of 10 × relatively to full HDM.
