k≤r
) order model as
V=[U1…Uk]
(19)
The SVD method has the benefit of being much faster to compute than the complete eigenproblem, while still having algorithms that retrieve the biggest eigenvalue in an iterative fashion. The power behind this whole process is that no assumptions are made on the original PDE itself, or on the properties of the ODE or solution that is reduced.49,50 In short, one can define the whole process behind the SVD–POD and like-minded methods to be:
1.	
Pose an optimization problem, of which the reduced basis V is the solution.
2.	
Discretize said problem over the considered space.
3.	
Generate the modal basis V from the discretized problem.
4.	
Solve the ROM.
Though this might seem just a repetition of something that can be seen from above, it is paramount to state it for it induces the questions that many researchers have made and tried to answer: is there a better problem to pose for a given application? What solution samples or “snapshots” should be taken? What norm should be used for the optimization problem? And how should the modal basis be created?
The reduced basis community seeks to consolidate projection methods, POD, snapshot, and basis generation into one mathematically solid package, with precise error bounds. Ohlberger and Rave29 give the advances and limitations of the reduced basis methods for reduced order modeling, mentioning shock-capturing methods and more interestingly methods to capture geometrical parameter dependence (for dynamic problems). These methods rely on a complete mathematical analysis of the PDE to be solved, making their generalization difficult.
Inner-product modification of the traditional POD problem can be done in the search for a better basis. One can, for example, use a change of variables, so that the POD expresses an energy that is conserved in the full-order problem;51 one can also modify the inner-product used for computing K and 
K^
. In other words, a symmetric positive-definite (or semi-definite for a demi norm) matrix M is chosen such that
K̂=∫t[w(t,μ1)…w(t,μnμ)]M[w(t,μ1)…w(t,μnμ)]Tdt
(20)
For the standard POD, M would be the identity matrix, but one could choose a semi-norm based on the outputs for the system, leading to the goal-oriented POD. Discussed in Carlberg and Farhat52 and presented in Bui-Thanh et al.,39 the idea is to go from a problem where the projection error of the state is minimized, as stated in equation (13), to minimizing the error of the output


12∑ing(w(μi),μi)-g(VVTw(μi),μi)‖2
(21)
To solve this problem, it is suggested in Carlberg and Farhat52 to compute the original problem in equation (13) but with the inner-product Θ given by
Θ=∂g∂wT∂g∂w,g=g(w(μ¯),μ¯)
(22)
Here 
μ¯
