12∑in∂g∂w(w-VVTw)‖2
(23)
To compute the problem, the SVD method can be used by recalculating the snapshot matrix as
Snew=Θ12Sold
(24)
One could also use an inner-product based on the Jacobian of f, called the system-induced inner-product
Θ=∂f∂wT∂f∂w,f=f(w(μ¯),μ¯)
(25)
The point of this inner-product is to also minimize the in-plane error. All of the derivatives are computed for the target parameters 
μ¯
 and the estimated solution given by equation (26). The authors do not seem to study the difference induced by either inner-product in their article. Bui-Thanh et al.39 generalize the idea exposed in Willcox et al.53 to nonlinear systems and go beyond changing the inner-product, stating a new problem to be minimized that mixes the model reduction technique and basis creation. It is shown that for linearized problems this method gives better results than just using POD, especially when the focused outputs are far less than the total number of states. However, no study of the computation time of the optimized basis is given, and no contrast is given between the total computation time of unmodified POD and the new method.
Adaptive finite-element snapshots, developed in Ullmann et al.54 allow for a POD of snapshots from triangular meshes exhibiting different degrees of refinement. Starting from triangular finite elements, the author provides a group of triangular meshes and shows how one can interpolate the function values of one mesh onto another, more refined common mesh. All snapshots would then be converted onto this common base. The author proposes a method where the ROM can still be created without generating this costly finite element space, where pairs of snapshots share a common mesh. It was shown both mathematically and by numerical experiments with an advection–diffusion equation and with a 2D diffusive Burgers' equation that this last method added a permanent error component related to the finite element discretization of the snapshots, while the method with the global common mesh did not. The Burguers equation used was a parabolic problem with adaptive refinement in time and the advection–diffusion equation was elliptic with different meshes for different parameter values. This may be useful for optimization, especially in transonic aerodynamics where a slight change in shape may be the difference between absence and existence of shocks. Unfortunately it restricts itself to 2D domains.
For the case of moving boundaries a strategy is given in Liberge and Hamdouni55: the fluid velocity field of the moving grid is interpolated onto a fixed uniform grid and the solid domain is represented in this grid by the fictitious domain method. Here the Navier–Stokes equations are extended to the rigid domain and a rigid constraint is added to the zone occupied by the rigid body. The authors used this with the snapshot POD to simulate the vibration induced by high Reynolds number (140,000) flow impacting a rigid cylinder attached by a spring to its origin. It was shown that the model error diminished with POD modes, and that with just six modes (therefore a sixth-order model) they match fairly well (with a relative error below 2%) the exact oscillation of the center of gravity. This could be useful for aeroelastic problems.
Compact POD
The compact POD brings the computed HDM sensitivities to the foray, by including them in the snapshot matrix. The method outlined in Carlberg and Farhat56 and further developed in Carlberg and Farhat52 is made specifically for steady systems. According to the authors, it provides a generalization of the original Lagrange-basis snapshot method into a Taylor one. In other words when discretizing the original problem in equation (14) into equation (15), the target solution is interpolated using other solution's derivatives relative to their parameters (meaning, their sensitivities) up to an arbitrary order and add these interpolations onto the snapshot set.
Suppose there exists p parameters, a precalculated solution 
w(μ)
 and its Jacobian relative to the parameter, and the solution of the target parameters 
μ¯
 is to be computed. From Taylor's theorem


w(μ¯)=w(μ)+∑i=1p∂w(μ)∂μi(μ¯i−μi)+O(||u¯−μ||22) 
(26)
Removing 
O(||u¯−μ||22)
 would give an approximate yet computable value of the target solution 
w(μ¯)
 which can be integrated into the snapshot matrix (see Carlberg and Farhat52,56 for the method). For n HDM samples, the full snapshot matrix is obtained by concatenating several individual snapshot matrices 
Si=S(μi)
 premultiplied by a Gaussian radial basis function. This gives more weight to snapshots closer to the desired state. The modal basis can then be computed via SVD.
The downside of this method is that the SVD must be recalculated each time and that additional computations must be done to construct the snapshot matrix.
Other methods57,58 considering snapshot sensitivities to improve reduced model accuracy have been applied to CFD problems. In Ito and Ravindran57 the authors developed a reduced order method which uses optimization techniques, while in Zimmermann58 a method that recurs to surrogate modeling was implemented.
Error estimation and control
For the Galerkin projection, the total error et can be decomposed in two components: the orthogonal error 
e⊥
