This estimator can then be used to address a known problem in greedy methods (mentioned in Paul-Dubois-Taine and Amsallem64 and Choi et al.65) using the residual as error estimators, which is that the residual might not reflect the proper scale of the error. The leave-one-out error estimates error for a region of parameter values, while this proposed method estimates the error for a given set of parameter values. This might be useful for optimization, where the optimum may be outside the region of parameter values initially used for the leave-one-out error estimator. The downside of this Jacobian estimator is the inversion of the Jacobian, which can be computationally costly.
Sampling
The problems to tackle in this section are how to choose the starting snapshots and how to further improve the modal basis by choosing the best snapshots possible. According to Carlberg et al.34 and Amsallem and Farhat,66 an initial method to gather samples would be to use uniform sampling (recommended for less than five parameters) or Latin Hypercube sampling (recommended for more than five parameters). Other than that, there are several strategies which are grouped in two types: greedy strategies, which choose samples that minimize an error measure of the ROM over the specified parameter space; and adaptive sampling, which seeks to minimize error along a trajectory through the parameter space, and not the overall space. Greedy methods are intended for offline–online strategies where one first constructs the best possible modal basis, and then the ROM is used to get the desired results. The most basic version searches for the parameter set that maximizes the residual, computes its snapshot, adds it to the snapshot set, and restarts the search until a certain criterion is reached.66 More advanced methods such as Haasdonk et al.67 or the ones presented next keep the principle, but try to avoid long sample generation times and over fitting. This is usually achieved by partitioning the parameter space, using alternative and faster methods to estimate error, and stopping the search after a certain, fixed number of samples is taken.
Optimization done using greedy methods may take more time than full HDM optimization. In Choi et al.,65 for one of the cases shown, a full HDM optimization took almost 1/50th of the time of the off-line basis building procedure. Adaptive sampling is on-line only: each call on the ROM might entail taking a new snapshot. It is therefore geared toward optimization. Unfortunately most methods found so far in the literature are oriented toward optimal control and affine parameter dependency. Examples of the former are the trust-region POD (TR-POD) given in Fahl and Sachs68 and optimality systems POD (OS-POD) given in Volkwein and Kunisch.69 TR-POD could be applied to parametric optimization (there is no specification of time or parameter dependency), but it requires the computation of a new snapshot and evaluation of the exact cost function at each step. This could be beneficial if one does not have the model sensibilities and is required to perform numerical differentiation to compute the gradient, which demands additional calls on the HDM under normal circumstances. However, most modern software already give the sensitivities required to compute the gradient of the cost function. To make this method useful one could “skip” some HDM evaluation steps. The second problem (affine parameter dependency) can be seen in Dihlmann and Haasdonk70: the authors manage to treat PDE operators that depend linearly on the parameters, but in the Navier–Stokes equations these correspond to fluid characteristics, which are not to be optimized. An interesting method relying on least-squares Petrov–Galerkin reduction is given in Zahr and Farhat,71 with good results for the inverse design of a subsonic profile: the total number of HDM calls for the inverse design procedure based on the algorithm was less than 1/4th of those for full HDM gradient-based optimization. This method is quite complex, relying TR-POD, OS-POD, and POD on state sensitivities to work. Work is reportedly being done to apply hyper-reduction based on this method, to gain additional speed-up.
Given that greedy methods can be readily applied to any class of problems no matter what type of parameter, some interesting greedy methods will be presented. Owing to the time cost of the SVD a method for fast SVD computations will be shown at the end.
Quad-tree exploration with leave-one-out error cross validation
Developed in Braconnier et al.,62 this greedy method uses the leave-one-out cross validation to estimate the error around the region of a snapshot and the global error of the modal basis. It then does a quad-tree split of the region with most error and generates new snapshots at the center of the new subregions. The method is tested on a few test functions and on the snapshots of a HDM computation of a RAE-2822 in a flight envelope with a subsonic–transonic transition. There was no calculation of the ROM, rather a Kriging interpolation of the reduced state variable was employed similar to Xiao et al.72 However, due to no reference to what technique gives us the reduced order solution, their method is fully applicable to actual ROM error reduction. According to the authors, the method allowed to greatly diminish surrogate model error relatively to other random sampling methods using the same number of snapshots all the while bringing about a 0.47% increase in computation time. It also managed to capture the transonic transition, which was not possible with the presented random parameter sampling methods.
The base building procedure is as follows:
1.	
Divide the space to explore recursively into 
2p
 regions, p being the number of parameters. Take snapshots at the center of each region.
2.	
Compute the local leave-one-out errors 
E(μi)
 and the global error E. If E is bigger than a certain tolerance, continue to the next step.
3.	
Find the parameter set μk with the biggest local error estimate and subdivide its corresponding region again into 
2p
 regions, and take snapshots at the center of each region. Freeze the original parameter set μk: no further refinement can be done for it, only on its children (thus preserving the quad-tree structure).
4.	
Repeat from step 2 until E is lower than the prescribed tolerance.
As it can be seen, this requires the computation of the several SVDs, where we add and take vectors from the snapshot set. To reduce the computation time the authors use the fast SVD downgrading and updating methods presented in Brand.63
Kriging-based greedy search
Presented in Paul-Dubois-Taine and Amsallem64 the method has three characteristics that make it stand aside from the rest:
1.	
The time to build the database is estimated, and a method is given so that the user can control how long the database building takes. In short, a maximum model order is defined, from which the maximum modal basis construction time and ROM solve time is deduced. The solve time for the HDM is assumed to be independent of the parameters. Then a cap is placed on the maximum of ROM and HDM solves and basis construction.
2.	
The random initial snapshot set is used to create a surrogate Kriging model for the error, and further candidate parameter sets are chosen based on the method of maximum probability of improvement on this surrogate. Upon each improvement cycle, an optimal cluster of parameters is selected from the original maximum probability improvement set and their snapshots computed. These snapshots are integrated into the ROM, the error indicator computed, and their error indicators integrated into the surrogate. Determining the best possible snapshots requires no ROM or HDM solves until the moment when a snapshot is generated and integrated into the database.
3.	
The exact error is computed by comparing the snapshot with the ROM solution for the same parameter values for the old and updated database. These exact error indicators are created at each search cycle and give a pool of exact indicators from which to build the surrogate error model.
As can be inferred from above, the algorithm has a certain complexity. Still, the usage of the surrogate model and optimal parameter clustering was shown to give consistently better convergence (error of the model decays faster) than a classical or random greedy algorithm for the given test case of the advection–diffusion equation of heat on a fractal domain (which is of interest for fluids). Additionally, for the structural problem of a blast under a V-hull, it was shown that for the same time budget the surrogate-error greedy method always gives a smaller error than the standard greedy procedure stated in the same article.
Precision
This section presents ways to ensure that the ROMs stay as close as possible to the original full-order models, seeking to preserve some information or part of the latter. It starts by presenting how one can, for dynamical systems, obtain a ROM whose dynamics are close to that of the original model. The reason behind this is that even though the idea is to work with stationary CFD, some solvers use time-marching methods. Time-marching methods solve a steady equation by simulating the response of the original dynamical system to a constant input. If the stability of this system is not preserved, the resulting ROM may diverge. Lastly, methods mixing reduced and full-order models are presented. These still speed up calculations while keeping the error as small as possible.
Stability preservation
For dynamical systems, POD-least mean squares tends to be dissipative and stable,37 but the POD-Galerkin can generate unstable models as shown in Rowley et al.51 The solution given by Rowley et al.51 is to choose an inner-product for the POD that is equivalent to an energy product, as it is demonstrated that in this way the stability of the original system is preserved. For compressible isentropic flows, the authors suggest using a change of variables for the POD inner-product so that it expresses the stagnation enthalpy.
An alternative far less complex approach is to use artificial viscosity. As it is noted in Weller et al.,37 Behzad,61 and Bergmann et al.73 using the stream-wise upwind Petrov Galerkin (SUPG) to model the Navier–Stokes equations allows the POD-Galerkin to faithfully reproduce the dynamics captured by the snapshots used to build it; it also allows it to remain stable and precise when the original direct numerical simulation (DNS) model was too coarse to faithfully capture the desired dynamics.61 Variational multiscale (VMS), of which SUPG is a particular case, can also be used, and is shown in Weller et al.37 and Bergmann et al.73 to give improved results at no additional computational cost. Both methods however need to be calibrated: SUPG requires the calibration of one parameter and VMS of two. In Behzad61 the artificial viscosity parameter of SUPG was based on the original article presenting the SUPG method74 while in Bergmann et al.73 the authors developed and employed a method that calibrates these parameters in a specific optimal fashion; they also point to Hughes et al.75 for a method that is model independent, requiring no calibration for either VMS or SUPG. It seems that the choice of parameter can affect the long term integration of the ROM: for a SUPG ROM in Behzad,61 a stalled airfoil would produce no CD and CL oscillations after the snapshot time interval (meaning the time interval where snapshots were taken) was exceeded. Instead, they converged to a mean value equal to the mean value of the HDM. In Bergmann et al.73 snapshots were taken for one vortex shedding cycle of a square cylinder. Here, both SUPG and VMS ROMs remained accurate for 40 vortex shedding cycles after the initial conditions, therefore well after the initial snapshot set. For the general case where one may use either RANS, large eddy simulation, or compressible Euler, Bergmann et al.73 present a review of stabilization methods that add dissipation directly to the reduced model in its ODE form.
Hybrid methods
Hybrid methods attempt to marry an HDM and a ROM so as to speed up calculations while preserving a precision comparable to that of the full-order model. A hybrid ROM achieves this marriage by reducing the part of the domain that changes the least with the parameters or boundary conditions being varied. An example can be found in Bergmann et al.76 to analyze the effects of wind gusts on an airfoil, the response of domain with no airfoil is calculated, and the corresponding snapshots are used to reduce only the far field of the full computation. Near the airfoil a full-order model is used, and the two domains are coupled via a Schwarz method.
Hybrid reduction by domain decomposition seems to have its origins in the report77 and later the article,73 where the authors speed-up by 20% the DNS calculations of a vortex-shedding square-cylinder, by using a POD-least mean squares ROM during 30% of the calculated time interval. But rather than using POD-least mean squares to reduce part of the time, Iollo et al.38 suggested using it to reduce part of the physical domain. In this case, the authors analyzed a convergent–divergent conduit with the compressible Euler equations. No shocks were present. The divergent end had its geometry and exit pressure varied, while the convergent end remained steady. The full domain was cut in two, overlapping domains: one for the convergent part and the other for the divergent one. For the former POD-least mean squares was used and for the latter a full solve was done each time. To get the complete answer, the two domains were coupled via the Schwarz method (see Dolean et al.78 for a brief presentation or the full book for a complete overview). The authors obtained relative (to the full HDM) errors on the state variables smaller than 0.5%. A way to build the required snapshot base was presented in Weller et al.,37 in which residual-based greedy sampling was combined with Voronoi tessellation. Under the European AeroGust (Aeroelastic Gust Modeling) program these methods were applied and further developed79,80 and finally presented in Bergmann et al.76 Here the authors applied it to a multi query analysis of an airfoil hit by gusts and optimization (drag-reduction) of the front bumper of a car. The former was done by DNS, the latter by RANS with Spalart–Allmaras turbulence model. For the airfoil a 65 × speed-up from full CFD to hybrid was observed all the while maintaining excellent accuracy (near-perfect match of CL curves). For the front bumper optimization, the hybrid model took 26% of the time (with snapshot generation included) of the full CFD optimization, and gave a drag reduction of 3.42%, while the full model gave one of 3.89%. For the optimization part, the authors recommend mesh morphing in lieu of re-meshing due to much faster execution times. Also, no adaptive or greedy sampling was used for the optimization. Developments are still under way, with sampling strategies being tested81 and considerable efficiency gains obtained with hybrid noncollocated meshes.82
Sub-structuring POD is presented in Radermacher et al.83 and shown in Radermacher and Reese84 alongside a new adaptive sub-structuring method (A-SPOD). Both these methods rely on Craig–Bampton85 sub-structuring to separate parts of a structure that deform in a primarily elastic manner, and those that do not. For the model reduction in itself the authors applied the POD-Galerkin method to the Newton–Raphson iterations encountered in structural mechanics. The authors tested their methods on the forming of a horizontal flute on sheet metal: in this process a robot presses down on a sheet of metal to bend it permanently along a horizontal line. They simulated this problem for several sheets of varying thickness using full-order models to obtain the snapshots. Then they applied SPOD and A-SPOD to a sheet whose thickness was different from the rest.
In both cases the calculations are done in less than half of the time of the HDM with relative errors for the displacement below 1% when more than 10 modes (ROM orders greater than 10) were used. Both methods have their disadvantages. A-SPOD requires continuous evaluation during solver iterations. SPOD requires the user to guess beforehand which zones will remain primarily elastic and which will not. Still, these methods seem useful for structures. Through SPOD, one could use a ROM where the deformation and the strains are the lowest, such as at the root of the wing, and using the HDM at the tip where they are the highest.
Reduction of benchmarks
In this paper, chosen model reduction methods will be tested on problems whose PDEs are close to those of the systems that the NOVEMOR MDO framework may try to optimize. The methods that will be tested are Galerkin projection with Standard POD, Galerkin projection with output/goal oriented POD (where the output Jacobian is available), Galerkin projection with system-induced POD (where the system Jacobian is available), least mean squares reduction (employing the Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno algorithm) with POD basis. The Galerkin and least mean squares are tested because they are the foundation of more advanced reduction methods (such as hyper-reduction or hybrid-ROMs). The different POD norms are tested to evaluate the effect of different norms on the ROM's accuracy.
The tests will be done on a dynamic linear advection problem, a steady linear advection problem, a steady nonlinear diffusion problem, and a dynamic linear oscillatory problem. The steady problems are solved for various parameter or input values, to justify the use of model reduction methods.
The dynamic problems will be used to test the stability of the Galerkin projection, given the possibility of instability. The steady parametric problems represent a simplified version of the problems one might find in CFD, while still displaying the same physics (advection or transport and diffusion). Thus, all applicable methods will be tested on the steady benchmarks.
Benchmarks
The benchmark problems used for this work can be found on the website of the Model-Order-Reduction-Wiki Community,86 where one can find the matrices required to simulate them. The dynamic advection problem (Problem 1) is described in Moosmann et al.87 and the matrices are available in The MORwiki Community.88 The 2D problem was chosen, which is a 2D thermal diffusion problem where a convective flow is applied to the domain. For the oscillatory problem the butterfly gyro problem was chosen (Problem 2), with its definition and data specified in Billger89 and Oberwolfach Benchmark Collection,90 respectively. This system is less damped than the dynamic advection problem, allowing for more severe tests of the ROM's stability. For the steady advection problem (Problem 3) the three-parameter anemometer model as described in Baur et al.91 and that is made available in The MORwiki Community92 was picked. For the nonlinear steady diffusion (Problem 4) case the benchmark available in Lienemann et al.93 and the MORwiki Community94 was used. This benchmark deals with the heat diffusion through a bar where heat diffusion varies with the temperature. All benchmark problems except the steady advection one (Problem 3) were compiled in the Oberwolfach Benchmark Collection.95 In Table 1 each benchmark is linked to the method tested on it.
