Here, L is the Lift, D is the Drag, Ω is the problem domain, p is the pressure, and i is the final iteration. These first two indicators will allow to quantify the error near the airfoil, rather than the whole domain, and are of great interest for aircraft. The last one is a stability indicator: if 
rpCauchy<1
 for any AoA, then the ROM is converging faster than the HDM, not displaying any stability problems.
Figures 7 and 8 depict the pressure coefficient obtained with ROM and HDM for a range of AoA varying from 0.5° to 4.5° (in a step of 0.5°), which include the four previously mentioned samples (1°, 2°, 3°, and 4°). Alongside the pressure coefficient, the error of ROM pressure relative to HDM is shown. The field error measures are available in Figure 9, the force error in Figure 10, and the ratio of Cauchy coefficients is available in Figure 11. Some key observations can be made from these graphics:
•	
For all AoA, the relative field error is consistently smaller than 1%.
•	
At the sampled AoA the field error abruptly decreases, but does not go to machine 0.
•	
The relative force error is around 10%, with no sudden decrease at the sampled AoA.
•	
The Cauchy ratio is always below 1.
•	
From the relative error plots (left column of Figures 7 and 8) one can clearly visualize where the ROM has more difficulties in capturing the physics (especially for the AoA not corresponding to the snapshots), which are where the pressure discontinuities occur, i.e. in the leading and trailing edges (especially in this last case).
