It seems that the ROM thus obtained is stable and, as observed for the lid-driven cavity, nearly perfectly matches the sampled points. However, it fails to give the aerodynamic forces with relative errors consistently below 1%. It could be then, that though the model well approximates most of the domain, its performance worsens near the airfoil. This issue could be tackled using hybrid models. Furthermore, at the previously sampled AoA, the relative errors (whether for aerodynamic forces or fields) do not go to machine 0. This could be due to the error linked to the time discretization (unaccounted for in the MOR employed) or linked to the dynamic behavior of the ROM.
Concluding remarks
A bibliographical research was done that established that for nonlinear problems, CFD in particular, the most common way to reduce models is to use Galerkin projection or least mean squares reduction. In order to see just how well the researched methods perform, simple problems with well-known outputs, and that were already reduced by other authors, were analyzed. It was observed that reduced models constructed by either the Galerkin projection or least mean squares method can give results with relative deviations below 10% in most cases. It was also made clear that the speed of the least mean squares method depends heavily on the performance optimization routines available in the software.
Once model reduction was applied to CFD models, deviations below 10% were observed, except when point singularities are present as seen in the lid-driven cavity problem. In this case the Galerkin projection showed deviations in pressure larger than 100% while those in velocity were below 1%. The least mean squares method revealed to be more robust and kept deviations below 10% for the most part. However, due to the optimization routines available in the used software, the least mean squares method failed to be faster than the original model, while the Galerkin projection managed to be up to nearly 3 × faster.
When finally applying the Galerkin projection to the compressible Euler simulation of a subsonic and supersonic airfoil flying at different AoA, one obtains deviations below 10% even when outside the original interval at which samples were taken. No loss of stability was observed when using reduced order modeling, even when simulating the compressible Euler equations, where no physical viscosity is present.
The work developed so far indicates that MOR may be able to provide faster models with acceptable errors for computations where parameters of a model are being varied. In particular, the 2D CFD models studied had either the AoA or Reynolds number varied, and the ROMs so produced were faster than the original model with reasonable errors.
